<h3>What this unit is about</h3>
<p>In 1641, a French philosopher named René Descartes published a short book that would reshape Western thought. The question driving it was deceptively simple: <em>Is there anything I can know for certain?</em> Not probably. Not usually. Certainly — beyond any possible doubt.</p>
<p>The <em>Meditations on First Philosophy</em> is the record of Descartes’ attempt to answer that question. Over six meditations, he strips away everything he has ever believed that could conceivably be wrong, searches for something that cannot be doubted, and then tries to rebuild knowledge on that foundation. What he finds — and what he builds from it — defines the terms of Western epistemology and metaphysics to this day.</p>
<h4>The six meditations, in outline</h4>
<p><strong>Meditation I</strong> launches the project. Descartes asks: could my senses be deceiving me right now? Could I be dreaming? Could an evil demon be feeding me false experiences about a world that doesn’t exist? He deliberately drives doubt as far as it will go, until almost nothing is left standing.</p>
<p><strong>Meditation II</strong> finds the one thing doubt cannot touch. Even if everything I believe about the world is wrong — even if there is an evil demon, even if I am dreaming — there is still <em>something doing the doubting</em>. <em>Cogito ergo sum</em>: I think, therefore I am. From this single certainty, Descartes begins the work of reconstruction.</p>
<p><strong>Meditations III–V</strong> attempt to establish the existence of God as a guarantee that clear and distinct perception is reliable. If God exists and is not a deceiver, then the faculty of reason Descartes has been using is trustworthy. These meditations include two arguments for God’s existence — one from causation, one from the nature of the concept of God itself — and address a famous puzzle about whether Descartes’ reasoning is circular.</p>
<p><strong>Meditation VI</strong> crosses the finish line. With God as guarantor, Descartes argues that the external world exists and that the mind and body are genuinely distinct substances. The mind is a thinking thing; the body is an extended thing; and they are, in some puzzling sense, joined. This conclusion — called <em>substance dualism</em> — will be contested, refined, and argued about for centuries after Descartes.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Why it matters</p>
<p>Descartes is often called the father of modern philosophy, and not just because he came early. He shifted philosophy’s central question from <em>What is the world?</em> to <em>How do we know anything at all?</em> That shift — from metaphysics to epistemology as the starting point — defines what comes after him. Hume, Hegel, and Kant are all, in different ways, responding to the problem Descartes identified.</p>
</div></div>
<div>
<p>← Looking back &nbsp;|&nbsp; Looking ahead →</p>
<p>In Unit 1, the <em>Euthyphro</em> raised the question of whether morality depends on divine authority. Descartes approaches the same relationship from the other direction: God guarantees not moral truth but <em>rational</em> truth — the reliability of reason itself. Ask yourself as you read: if God were necessary for reason to work, what would that mean for us now?</p>
<p>In Unit 3, David Hume will challenge the very faculty Descartes is trying to vindicate. Where Descartes relies on reason to establish what we can know, Hume will argue that reason alone tells us almost nothing — and that even cause and effect is just a habit of the mind.</p>
</div>
