<h3>Unit reading</h3>
<p>This unit has one text: the complete <em>Meditations on First Philosophy</em>. At about 50 pages in translation, it is the longest reading in the course — but it is structured to be read one meditation at a time, and each meditation is short. Plan to read it in at least two or three sittings, and in order.</p>
<h4>Primary text</h4>
<div>
<p>René Descartes, <em>Meditations on First Philosophy</em></p>
<p>Trans. Jonathan Bennett — Early Modern Texts (free, openly accessible)</p>
<a href="https://www.earlymoderntexts.com/assets/pdfs/descartes1641.pdf" target="_blank" class="download-link">→ Download PDF (earlymoderntexts.com)</a></div>
<h4>A note on reading the Meditations</h4>
<p>Descartes writes in first person, in a style he calls “meditation” — meaning he is narrating a process of inquiry, not delivering conclusions. The <em>I</em> in the text is a deliberate construction: a meditator stripping away assumptions. You are not being told what to think; you are invited to follow along and test each move yourself.</p>
<p>Descartes uses a technical phrase — <strong>clear and distinct perception</strong> — to describe the kind of certainty he is looking for. Pay attention to when and how he uses it. By Meditation VI, it does a lot of work.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
